WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.860
You might already be using AI on
the farm without calling it that.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.230
Let's walk round the
place and pick it out.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.270
Start with the herd.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:12.510
Collars and ear tags
track movement, grazing,

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:15.750
rumination and rest, day and night.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:18.817
Software learns what normal
looks like for each cow,

00:00:18.837 --> 00:00:21.323
and flags her when something changes.

00:00:21.343 --> 00:00:25.640
Heat, a health problem, or
her rumination dropping off.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.360
Virtual fencing collars
go one step further.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:34.160
They use GPS and a sound cue to keep
stock inside a line you draw on your

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.387
phone.

00:00:35.407 --> 00:00:37.333
They started on dairy farms.

00:00:37.353 --> 00:00:40.530
Now more and more beef
farms are taking them up.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:46.250
In the yards, EID tags and scales
record every animal's weight.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.650
Much of that is plain automation.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.717
A reading goes in, a number comes out.

00:00:51.737 --> 00:00:54.810
The AI part is when the
software predicts something,

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:58.380
like the day each lamb
will reach killing weight.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.980
Out on the paddocks, satellite pasture
services estimate your cover from

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.980
space, paddock by paddock, every few days.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.033
That's prediction, built from
images and past readings.

00:01:11.053 --> 00:01:14.207
It can be off under cloud,
or in steep country.

00:01:14.227 --> 00:01:17.670
So a plate meter walk
still earns its place.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.950
Weather forecasts lean on
AI more each year too.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:26.750
The big forecasting centres now run
AI weather models alongside the older

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:28.110
physics models.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:30.830
A good forecast gives you a range.

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.337
Seventy percent chance of rain is a guess,

00:01:33.357 --> 00:01:35.520
with the odds written on it.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:40.040
What's new since about
2023 is the third kind,

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.680
AI that writes and talks.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.730
It can draft an email to the stock

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.850
agent, read a long consent
document and pull out the

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:52.410
conditions, or turn your voice
notes into a tidy diary.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:56.290
Some tools now go a step
further and do jobs for you,

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:59.037
like booking or filling in forms.

00:01:59.057 --> 00:02:02.340
That's early days, so watch what they do.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.820
When a rep tells you a
product has AI in it,

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.220
three questions cut through.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.540
What does it sense or predict?

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.007
From what data?

00:02:12.027 --> 00:02:14.270
And how often is it wrong?

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:17.243
A good rep will have straight answers.

00:02:17.263 --> 00:02:20.950
And whatever the tools say, keep
your own eyes on the stock.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:24.120
You know your animals and your country.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.360
Next, we'll look at
what chat AI does well,

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.490
and where it gets things wrong.
